  "content": {
    "id": "v2-c5c49-p14-practice-slot-2-upper-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Jess says:\n\nThe inverse of an odd function will always be a function.\n\nAn example is $y=x^3$.\n\nShow that she is incorrect by giving a counterexample of the form $y=x^3-kx$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$f(x)=x^3-3x$ is odd, but $f(1)=f(-2)=-2$. It fails the horizontal line test, so its inverse is not a function.",
      "worked": "Take $k=3$, so $f(x)=x^3-3x$.\n$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=(-x)^3-3(-x)\\\\&=-x^3+3x\\\\&=-f(x)\\end{align*}$$\nThus $f$ is odd. However,\n$$\\begin{align*}f(1)&=1-3=-2\\\\f(-2)&=-8+6=-2\\end{align*}$$\nso the horizontal line $y=-2$ meets the graph at least twice. The function is not one-to-one, so its inverse is not a function.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": null
  },
